Part 1 — Create your GCP project

Go to:

Google Cloud Console

Create a new project.

For example:

Project name:
kong-learning

Google Cloud requires a project for Compute Engine resources, and its current documentation recommends enabling the Compute Engine API before creating a VM.

Part 2 — Enable Compute Engine

Open:

APIs & Services → Library

Search for:

Compute Engine API

Click:

Enable

Or use Cloud Shell:

gcloud services enable compute.googleapis.com

Check:

gcloud services list --enabled

You should see:

compute.googleapis.com
Part 3 — Choose a region

For learning, choose a region close to your users.

For example:

asia-south1

This is Mumbai.

Or:

asia-south2

Delhi.

For the rest of this guide, I'll use:

REGION=asia-south1
ZONE=asia-south1-a

Set them:

gcloud config set compute/region asia-south1
gcloud config set compute/zone asia-south1-a

Check:

gcloud config list
Part 4 — Create the VM

We'll use:

VM name: kong-server
Machine: e2-medium
OS: Ubuntu 24.04 LTS
Disk: 30 GB

Google's current Compute Engine documentation supports Ubuntu 24.04 LTS for Linux VM creation.

Run:

gcloud compute instances create kong-server \
    --zone=asia-south1-a \
    --machine-type=e2-medium \
    --image-family=ubuntu-2404-lts-amd64 \
    --image-project=ubuntu-os-cloud \
    --boot-disk-size=30GB \
    --boot-disk-type=pd-balanced \
    --tags=kong-server

Wait for:

NAME          ZONE           MACHINE_TYPE
kong-server   asia-south1-a e2-medium
Part 5 — Create firewall rules

We need to allow traffic to Kong.

Kong's standard ports include:

8000  → Proxy HTTP
8443  → Proxy HTTPS
8001  → Admin API
8444  → Admin API HTTPS

The Google Cloud firewall rules determine which incoming traffic reaches the VM.

Allow Kong HTTP

Run:

gcloud compute firewall-rules create allow-kong-http \
    --network=default \
    --direction=INGRESS \
    --action=ALLOW \
    --rules=tcp:8000 \
    --source-ranges=0.0.0.0/0 \
    --target-tags=kong-server
Allow Kong HTTPS
gcloud compute firewall-rules create allow-kong-https \
    --network=default \
    --direction=INGRESS \
    --action=ALLOW \
    --rules=tcp:8443 \
    --source-ranges=0.0.0.0/0 \
    --target-tags=kong-server
Allow Admin API

For learning, you can temporarily allow port 8001.

Do not do this in production.

gcloud compute firewall-rules create allow-kong-admin \
    --network=default \
    --direction=INGRESS \
    --action=ALLOW \
    --rules=tcp:8001 \
    --source-ranges=0.0.0.0/0 \
    --target-tags=kong-server

Later we'll restrict this.

Part 6 — Get the VM external IP

Run:

gcloud compute instances describe kong-server \
    --zone=asia-south1-a \
    --format='get(networkInterfaces[0].accessConfigs[0].natIP)'

You'll get something like:

34.93.120.50

Let's call this:

KONG_IP=34.93.120.50
Part 7 — SSH into the VM

Run:

gcloud compute ssh kong-server \
    --zone=asia-south1-a

You should now be inside your VM:

username@kong-server:~$

Everything from here happens inside the GCP VM unless I say otherwise.

Part 8 — Update Ubuntu

Run:

sudo apt update

Then:

sudo apt upgrade -y

Install utilities:

sudo apt install -y \
    curl \
    git \
    ca-certificates \
    gnupg \
    lsb-release
Part 9 — Install Docker

For this learning setup:

sudo apt install -y docker.io docker-compose-v2

Check:

docker --version

and:

docker compose version

You should get something like:

Docker version ...
Docker Compose version ...

Start Docker:

sudo systemctl enable docker
sudo systemctl start docker

Check:

sudo systemctl status docker

You want:

Active: active (running)
Part 10 — Allow your user to use Docker

Run:

sudo usermod -aG docker $USER

Then:

newgrp docker

Test:

docker run hello-world

If you get:

Hello from Docker!

Docker is working.

Part 11 — Create Kong directory

Run:

mkdir ~/kong

Then:

cd ~/kong

Check:

pwd

You should have:

/home/your-user/kong
Part 12 — Create Docker Compose file

Run:

nano docker-compose.yml

Paste:

services:

  kong-database:
    image: postgres:16
    container_name: kong-database
    restart: unless-stopped

    environment:
      POSTGRES_USER: kong
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: kongpassword
      POSTGRES_DB: kong

    volumes:
      - kong_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data

    networks:
      - kong-net


  kong-migrations:
    image: kong:latest
    container_name: kong-migrations

    command: kong migrations bootstrap

    environment:
      KONG_DATABASE: postgres
      KONG_PG_HOST: kong-database
      KONG_PG_USER: kong
      KONG_PG_PASSWORD: kongpassword
      KONG_PG_DATABASE: kong

    depends_on:
      - kong-database

    networks:
      - kong-net


  kong:
    image: kong:latest
    container_name: kong
    restart: unless-stopped

    environment:
      KONG_DATABASE: postgres
      KONG_PG_HOST: kong-database
      KONG_PG_USER: kong
      KONG_PG_PASSWORD: kongpassword
      KONG_PG_DATABASE: kong

      KONG_PROXY_ACCESS_LOG: /dev/stdout
      KONG_ADMIN_ACCESS_LOG: /dev/stdout

      KONG_PROXY_ERROR_LOG: /dev/stderr
      KONG_ADMIN_ERROR_LOG: /dev/stderr

      KONG_ADMIN_LISTEN: 0.0.0.0:8001

    ports:
      - "8000:8000"
      - "8443:8443"
      - "8001:8001"
      - "8444:8444"

    depends_on:
      kong-migrations:
        condition: service_completed_successfully

    networks:
      - kong-net


volumes:
  kong_data:


networks:
  kong-net:

Save:

CTRL + O
ENTER
CTRL + X

This follows the same basic Docker Compose architecture documented by Kong: PostgreSQL, Kong migrations, and the Kong Gateway container.

Part 13 — Start PostgreSQL

Run:

docker compose up -d kong-database

Check:

docker ps

You should see:

kong-database

Check the logs:

docker logs kong-database

Look for PostgreSQL being ready.

Part 14 — Run Kong database migration

Now run:

docker compose run --rm kong-migrations

This initializes the Kong PostgreSQL schema.

You should eventually see migration completion.

Kong's documented Docker Compose deployment uses kong migrations bootstrap to initialize the database schema.

Part 15 — Start Kong

Run:

docker compose up -d kong

Check:

docker ps

You should have:

kong
kong-database
Part 16 — Check Kong locally

Inside your GCP VM:

curl http://localhost:8001

You should get JSON.

Something similar to:

{
  "version": "...",
  "database": {
    "reachable": true
  }
}

Kong's documentation uses a request to port 8001 to validate that the Gateway is running.

Part 17 — Test Kong from your computer

Remember the external IP:

34.93.120.50

From your own laptop:

curl http://34.93.120.50:8001

You should get Kong's JSON response.

If you don't, check:

docker ps

and:

sudo ss -lntp

Also check the GCP firewall:

gcloud compute firewall-rules list
Part 18 — Create your first Kong Service

Now we're going to actually use Kong.

We'll use httpbin.org as a temporary backend.

Inside the GCP VM:

curl -i -X POST http://localhost:8001/services \
    --data name=httpbin \
    --data url=https://httpbin.org

You should receive a 201 Created.

Check:

curl http://localhost:8001/services

You should see:

httpbin
Part 19 — Create your first Kong Route

Now create:

/httpbin

Run:

curl -i -X POST \
    http://localhost:8001/services/httpbin/routes \
    --data 'paths[]=/httpbin'

Now your architecture is:

                    GCP
                     │
                     ▼
              Kong :8000
                     │
                /httpbin
                     │
                     ▼
               httpbin.org
Part 20 — Test it

From your laptop:

curl http://34.93.120.50:8000/httpbin/get

You should get a JSON response from httpbin.

🎉 At this point you have:

Google Cloud
    │
    ▼
Compute Engine
    │
    ▼
Docker
    │
    ▼
Kong Gateway
    │
    ▼
httpbin

You've successfully deployed Kong on GCP.

Part 21 — Learn Kong Service and Route

Now understand these two concepts really well.

Service
httpbin
       │
       ▼
https://httpbin.org

The Service represents the upstream application.

Route
/httpbin

The Route determines how the client reaches the Service.

So:

Client

GET
http://34.93.120.50:8000/httpbin/get

          │
          ▼

        Kong

          │
          │ Route: /httpbin
          ▼

       httpbin

          │
          ▼

https://httpbin.org/get

This is the fundamental Kong architecture.

Part 22 — Add API Key authentication

Now let's make the API secure.

Create a Consumer:

curl -i -X POST \
    http://localhost:8001/consumers \
    --data username=test-user

Create an API key:

curl -i -X POST \
    http://localhost:8001/consumers/test-user/key-auth \
    --data key=my-secret-key

Enable API key authentication:

curl -i -X POST \
    http://localhost:8001/services/httpbin/plugins \
    --data name=key-auth

Now try:

curl http://34.93.120.50:8000/httpbin/get

It should fail with an authentication error.

Now send the key:

curl \
    -H "apikey: my-secret-key" \
    http://34.93.120.50:8000/httpbin/get

Now it should work.

Your architecture has become:

Client
   │
   │ API Key
   ▼
Kong
   │
   ├── Authenticate
   │
   ▼
Backend
Part 23 — Add rate limiting

Now protect the API from excessive requests.

Run:

curl -i -X POST \
    http://localhost:8001/services/httpbin/plugins \
    --data name=rate-limiting \
    --data config.minute=10

Now Kong limits the service to approximately:

10 requests / minute

This is one of the major reasons to put Kong in front of APIs.

Part 24 — Add CORS

If your frontend is running in a browser, you may need CORS.

Example:

curl -i -X POST \
    http://localhost:8001/services/httpbin/plugins \
    --data name=cors \
    --data 'config.origins=*'

For production, don't blindly use:

*

Instead configure your real frontend domain.

For example:

https://app.example.com
