Part 26 — Important: secure the Admin API

Do not leave this:

8001 → 0.0.0.0/0

on a production system.

Port 8001 is the Kong Admin API.

It should be accessible only from trusted management locations.

For example, find your current firewall rule:

gcloud compute firewall-rules list

Then remove the public rule:

gcloud compute firewall-rules delete allow-kong-admin

For learning, you can instead create a rule that only allows your public IP:

gcloud compute firewall-rules create allow-kong-admin-my-ip \
    --network=default \
    --direction=INGRESS \
    --action=ALLOW \
    --rules=tcp:8001 \
    --source-ranges=YOUR_PUBLIC_IP/32 \
    --target-tags=kong-server

Replace:

YOUR_PUBLIC_IP

with your actual public IP.

Part 27 — Don't expose PostgreSQL

Notice that our Docker Compose file does not publish:

5432:5432

to the internet.

That's intentional.

Your database should remain internal:

Kong
  │
  │ Docker network
  ▼
PostgreSQL

not:

Internet
   │
   ▼
PostgreSQL :5432

Never expose PostgreSQL publicly just to make Kong work.

Part 28 — Production GCP architecture

Once you've learned the above, I would not use a single VM for production.

A more realistic architecture is:

                         Internet
                            │
                            ▼
                   Cloud DNS
                            │
                            ▼
               Google Cloud HTTPS LB
                            │
                            ▼
                   ┌────────────────┐
                   │      Kong      │
                   │                │
                   │   Kong #1      │
                   │   Kong #2      │
                   │   Kong #3      │
                   └───────┬────────┘
                           │
                           ▼
                    Cloud SQL
                    PostgreSQL
                           │
                           │
             ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
             ▼             ▼             ▼
         User API      Order API     Payment API

You can use:

Compute Engine or GKE for Kong
Cloud SQL PostgreSQL for Kong's database
Cloud Load Balancing in front
Cloud DNS
Google-managed SSL certificates
Secret Manager
Cloud Monitoring
Cloud Logging
private networking
multiple Kong instances
